{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head ed81b9d47a9763a0dadce9a25129101ca569750c on its S6 branch, all 40 artifacts, for request 72cbb28b under my promise 5b84f67d. The implementation is S6 (request 1c4557ec, promise 2de78527). The reporting artifact is the S6 note.\n\nThe head is 6 commits on origin main d58ff62f, and it fast-forwards main. `git merge-tree --write-tree d58ff62f ed81b9d4` gives tree b5b807e7, the head's own tree. `check-commit-messages d58ff62f..ed81b9d4` checks 6 commits and flags none. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `git diff --check` is clean, and no added line names a local path or another project's Worker.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **The gate.** The gate passed on source 77abe321 (tree c437ee73, 0 changed files, exit 0, 1,371.18 s). The review head differs from 77abe321 in the note alone (tree b5b807e7). `gate.txt` hashes to `cd27bd7b…`. The note keeps both failed gates with their own logs and hashes, and the second one found the real Page fault that the final source fixes.\n- **The note's hashes.** All 31 hashes in the note's tables and prose match the retained files, including the two failed gates, the preflight and the OAuth popup runs.\n- **Scope.** The 40 artifacts filed at this head equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only d58ff62f ed81b9d4`.\n- **Version rule 163de663.** Counter 0.8.0 has no release directory, and the signed line ends at 0.7.0. Only the open 0.8.0 presentation identifier moves. No release packet and no trust row changes.\n\n## The six findings\n\n1. **Terminal text.** `shownText` in `packages/cli/src/shown-text.ts` uses the character classes of `shownPath` in `packages/git/src/diff.ts` (C0, DEL, backslash, C1, bidi controls, line and paragraph separators). `shownJson` keeps the JSON framing and escapes only the extra controls. `dap issues` and the other member-text sites that I read escape on output and change no stored value. The red control for `dap issues` is distinguished, and the test feeds a title with a newline and a bidi control and asserts one safe line.\n2. **Clone remote.** `credentialRemote` requires the exact recorded host, namespace and name: `https://github.com/\u003cns\u003e/\u003cname\u003e.git`, or the `/git/\u003cns\u003e/\u003cname\u003e.git` path for `artifacts`. It also refuses a URL that contains the token, raw or decoded. `finishCredentialClone` runs it before the save and before Git, and the refusal says the single-use credential may be consumed. The red control removes the check and the test fails by its name. The archive path is unchanged.\n3. **Opener policy.** `apps/room/fetch.ts` classifies the Page route after the raw URL bound, and it sets `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups` on every admitted Page answer, including the 301, 405, 400 and 404 answers. A 413 for an oversized URL carries no header, because that request is never classified. This is the fault the second gate found. The route test checks seven statuses, and the control that deletes the header fails it by name. `/oauth/authorize` stays unchanged. Planner rulings be8d9c03 and 499fb212 set this: Chrome for Testing 153 showed that the header on the consent page severs the popup callback's opener, so the ruling adds no header there. The note records the measurement, the two controls and the limits (an error callback, no successful grant, no same-tab control run). Page source uses no `window.open` or `window.opener`.\n4. **Artifacts member read mint.** `mintRead` now compares `info().remote` with the exact remote before it mints, and it requires `scope === \"read\"` on the reply. A missing scope and a `write` scope fail, and the mint answer holds the plaintext only as before. The red log fails at the old source and the green log passes five cases, including the local simulator origin.\n5. **Workspace plaintext.** `WorkspaceExpiry` keeps one row for each unread plaintext, maintained by triggers on insert, on nulling and on delete. A bounded alarm quantum (64 rows) nulls the plaintext at `ends`. It never revokes anything: revocation uses the token ID, and `#take` already refuses a token whose end has passed. A backfill pass covers legacy rows, 64 for each alarm, in `(scope, token)` order. The native test uses fresh and pre-queue rows, the exact end, a restart and the real `ScopeObject` alarm. The three new tables sit in `room-format.ts` and in the table-history fixture. The seals for `object.ts` and the new module are in the inventory.\n6. **Spent invitation.** The CLI saves a terminal `invitation-used` or `invitation-expired` refusal, then drops the private request. A lost reply, another refusal, an unverified acceptance or an inactive inbox keeps the request. After a verified completion it writes a `complete` marker before it drops the request, so an interrupted cleanup resumes with no new submission. Three CLI tests cover the spent refusal, the interrupted cleanup and the resume. Counter keeps the fragment while an attempt runs and clears only the matching fragment when the attempt fails or custody is saved. The helper keeps the path, query, history state and any other fragment.\n\n## Wake rate\n\nThe head adds one wake source: the workspace expiry queue shares the existing credential-expiry slot in `Wakes`, and the earlier of the two wins.\n- **Floor:** 0 added requests per hour. With no unread workspace plaintext, or no `private_workspace_token` table, `wake()` returns null and sets nothing.\n- **Per room that mints:** at most one wake for each minted token that nobody read, at that token's end. A token lives at most 900 s, and the wakes coalesce into the earliest, so the count follows the room's own accepted mint steps and never idle time.\n- **One time:** a room with legacy rows runs one backfill alarm for each 64 rows, then stops.\n- **Failure:** if the expiry statement itself keeps failing, the due row keeps the wake at \"now\", as the credential-expiry queue already does. The statement has no data-dependent failure that I found.\n\n## Non-blocking\n\n- **Stale dated note.** `notes/2026-11-01-git-read-token-after-revocation.md` (line 292) still says that `private_workspace_token` is outside the expiry queue and that an unread plaintext stays until a read or a revocation clears it. This head makes that sentence false, and its cited line numbers have moved. Correct it in a notes-only head, with the planner's design-note status pass.\n- **Counter input box.** The invitation box no longer fills from the address fragment, and Join and Check use the held fragment when the box is empty. The note does not state that visible change.\n- **Reds for items 5 and 6b.** The workspace and Counter-fragment witnesses fail at the old source because the modules they import do not exist there. The note records no run of that failure. I accept the structural red.\n- **Display coverage is a list.** No test stops a new command from printing member text raw. My sweep of `${...handle|title|text|label|member|values[...}` finds only typed native values and one resume line (`Joined as ${before.handle}` in `command-membership.ts`, a handle that the native guard already admitted).\n- **Evidence gaps the note states.** Two commands (workspace test and invitation focused run) are not recorded exactly. Their logs and hashes are retained.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Each fix sits at its existing boundary (CLI output, clone, Worker entry, provider mint, host custody, join store), reuses the existing expiry and diagnosis shapes, and adds no entry kind, reason code or authority.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The remote check binds the credential to the recorded repository before any disclosure. The mint refuses a wrong remote or scope. The opener header is limited to measured Page routes. Local plaintext leaves at its end and revocation stays owed under the existing provider workflow, as the note says. A spent invitation secret leaves the private store only after a terminal refusal or a verified completion.\n\nSimplification: no required change. `shownJson` and `shownText` are two small helpers with one rule, and `WorkspaceExpiry` mirrors the credential-expiry queue.\n\nFacts for the planner's deployment: this head changes the scope Durable Object (`object.ts`), the room Worker (`fetch.ts`, regenerated Page assets), the CLI, and Counter's open 0.8.0 screen. Every room Worker needs the deployment and a DO-rate read. The new SQLite tables and triggers are additive, so an older Worker keeps working against them. The Counter fragment change reaches people only after the Counter screen is republished with its new open presentation identifier.\n\nThis approval authorizes no push, landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:84a82d6619775e500f61dca47cc239e873c701ad","binding":"assigned","head":"ed81b9d47a9763a0dadce9a25129101ca569750c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5b84f67d0b0df06097fdb8eb778e6f66c35ea82b\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2de785274654e85c14be9398b01b7457a5421e02\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7b256275394561d599674a02cc4163d254149e2a","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}